## Structures in C

A structure is a collection of variables that are grouped together to form a single entity.

```c
struct person {
    char name[100];
    int status;
};
```

The above code defines a structure named person with two variables name and status.

To access the variables in a structure, you need to use the dot operator.

```c
struct person p;

strcpy(p.name, "John");
p.status = 0;
```

## Arrays in C

An array is a collection of elements of the same type that are stored in contiguous memory locations.

```c
int arr[5];
```

The above code defines an array named arr with 5 elements of type int.

To access the elements in an array, you need to use the subscript operator.

```c
int arr[5];

arr[0] = 10;
arr[1] = 20;
arr[2] = 30;
arr[3] = 40;
arr[4] = 50;
```

## Pointers in C

A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable.

```c
int x = 10;
int *p = &x;
```

The above code defines a variable named x with value 10 and a pointer named p that stores the memory address of x.

To access the value stored at a pointer, you need to use the dereference operator.

```c
int x = 10;
int *p = &x;

printf("%d\n", *p);
```

Output:

```
10
```

## Dynamic Memory Allocation in C

Dynamic memory allocation is the process of allocating memory dynamically at runtime.

```c
int *p = (int *) malloc(sizeof(int));
```

The above code allocates memory for an integer and stores the memory address of the allocated memory in the pointer p.

To free the memory allocated by malloc, you need to use the free function.

```c
int *p = (int *) malloc(sizeof(int));

free(p);
```

