To get started, create a file called `print_arg.c` and open it in your editor.

Next, write a `main()` function that takes two arguments: `argc` and `argv`. `argc` is the number of arguments passed to the program, and `argv` is an array of strings containing the arguments.

## 1.1.2. Getting the Argument

Next, write a `for` loop that iterates over the elements in `argv`. Inside the loop, check if the index of the current element in `argv` matches the index passed as an argument to the program. If it does, print the corresponding element from `argv`.

## 1.1.3. Running the Program

Finally, compile and run the program to make sure it works. To compile and run the program, use the following command:

```bash
$ gcc print_arg.c -o print_arg
$./print_arg 1
hello
```

## 1.1.4. Solution

Here is the complete solution to the exercise:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // given an index in argv[1], return the corresponding argument from argv[]
    for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
        if (i == atoi(argv[1])) {
            printf("%s\n", argv[i]);
        }
    }
}
```

